sd-event: block SIGCHLD when a child is watched without a pidfd

Completes 0003. That patch restores the SIGCHLD path for kernels without
pidfd_open(), but the path is only half-connected: nothing blocks SIGCHLD, and
a signalfd receives nothing while its signal is unblocked.

verify_sigchld() demands a blocked SIGCHLD only for WSTOPPED/WCONTINUED. For a
plain WEXITED watch it does not, because upstream expects such a watch to run
off a pidfd and never involve the signal at all. Callers written against that
contract -- systemd-udevd among them -- leave SIGCHLD unblocked. Read from the
running device, attempt 16:

    /proc/351/status   SigBlk: 0008000000004003    (SIGHUP, SIGINT, SIGALRM,
                       SigIgn: 0000000180000000     SIGTERM, RT52 -- no SIGCHLD)
                       SigCgt: 0000000000000000

So worker exits were delivered as an unblocked, uncaught SIGCHLD, whose default
action is to be discarded. The event loop never learned, and the effect was
subtle rather than total: sd_event_add_child() sets need_process_child = true
itself, so every *new* worker caused process_child() to sweep and reap whichever
earlier workers had since exited. Reaping therefore tracked worker creation, and
the final batch was never reaped at all.

Measured, attempt 16 (patches 0002-0004, udev otherwise working):

  - 1061 devices in /run/udev/data and 326 in /run/udev/links, so events were
    genuinely processed;
  - 14 zombie (udev-worker) processes parented to udevd, permanent;
  - /run/udev/queue never removed, so `udevadm settle` timed out at 10 s
    despite there being no real work left.

Blocking SIGCHLD is what sd_event_add_child()'s callers were historically
required to do, and it is inherited across fork(). udev workers use waitid() on
the programs they spawn, which is unaffected by the mask.

--- a/src/libsystemd/sd-event/sd-event.c
+++ b/src/libsystemd/sd-event/sd-event.c
@@ -1690,6 +1690,19 @@
 
         } else {
                 /* We shall wait for some other event than WEXITED */
+
+                if (s->child.pidfd < 0) {
+                        /* verify_sigchld() only demands a blocked SIGCHLD for WSTOPPED/WCONTINUED,
+                         * because a WEXITED watch is expected to run off a pidfd and never touch the
+                         * signal at all. Without a pidfd it does: the exit reaches this event loop only
+                         * through the signalfd, and a signalfd sees nothing while the signal is
+                         * unblocked. Callers written against pidfd semantics -- systemd-udevd among them
+                         * -- therefore leave SIGCHLD unblocked, so block it here. */
+                        r = sigprocmask_many(SIG_BLOCK, NULL, SIGCHLD);
+                        if (r < 0)
+                                return r;
+                }
+
                 r = event_make_signal_data(e, SIGCHLD, NULL);
                 if (r < 0)
                         return r;
